course: dictionary
lesson: 2282
title: One word, many meanings: creole
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A creole is a language that grew when people who spoke different languages lived together. It started as a mix, then became the first language of a whole group of people. «Haitian Creole, which grew from French and African languages» is a real, full language with its own grammar, not broken English or French.
explain: One word can point to a language, a person, or food. Use the clues around it to decide which one. «Jamaican Patois, also called Jamaican Creole» tells you it is a language. «Creole cooking» tells you it is food, because cooking is about food.
explain: Check the sentence, then swap in your meaning and see if it still fits. Creole can also be an adjective, describing something, as in «Creole food» or «a French-based creole».
fact: Creole means a language that grew from a mix of two or more languages and became the first language of a group of people.
fact: «Haitian Creole» and «Jamaican Patois» are examples of creole languages.
fact: Creole can also mean a person of mixed European and African or local descent in the Caribbean or Louisiana.
fact: «Creole cooking» is spicy food from Louisiana in the USA, with rice, peppers and seafood.
fact: Creoles show how new languages are born when people who speak different languages live together.
exercise: «Haitian Creole, which grew from French and African languages.» What is Haitian Creole? | A type of bread | A kind of spicy rice | A person who lives in Louisiana | *A language that grew from a mix of languages
exercise: «Creole cooking» means what? | A school subject about languages | *Spicy food from Louisiana, with rice, peppers and seafood | A type of bread from Paris | A cold drink from the Caribbean
exercise: In «Creole cooking», the word Creole describes the food. What part of speech is it? | A question word | A noun for a place | *An adjective | A verb
test: «Mi soon come» is said in Jamaican Creole. What does it mean? | Please come here now | I have already gone home | Do not come at all | *I'll be there soon, though maybe later
test: Why is a creole called a real language and not broken English? | *It has its own grammar | It is only spoken by one person | It uses no words from any other language | It is written only in pictures
exam: Which sentence uses creole to mean a language? | «A Creole family lives in the next village.» | «She cooked a Creole dish with peppers.» | *«Haitian Creole grew from French and African languages.» | «Creole cooking is served at many restaurants.»
